description:
Implement pure lexical (filesystem-free) path normalization. This replaces the current `paths_equivalent` in `src/main.rs`, which calls `Path::canonicalize()` — unusable in an orchestrator because the server must reason about paths on filesystems it cannot see.

Create `crates/transcodarr-core/src/paths/normalize.rs` with:

```rust
/// Lexically normalise a path with no filesystem access.
pub fn lexically_normalize(path: &camino::Utf8Path) -> camino::Utf8PathBuf;

/// True when two paths normalise to the same value. No filesystem access.
pub fn paths_equivalent(a: &camino::Utf8Path, b: &camino::Utf8Path) -> bool;

/// True when `candidate` is `root` or lies underneath it, compared
/// component-wise after normalisation.
pub fn is_within(root: &camino::Utf8Path, candidate: &camino::Utf8Path) -> bool;

/// Number of path components after normalisation. Used to rank mount prefixes.
pub fn component_count(path: &camino::Utf8Path) -> usize;
```

Normalisation rules, all mandatory:
- Drop `.` components.
- Resolve `..` against the immediately preceding normal component.
- On an absolute path, a `..` that would rise above `/` is dropped (`/a/../..` -> `/`).
- On a relative path, leading `..` components are preserved (`../a/../b` -> `../b`).
- Collapse repeated separators (`/a//b` -> `/a/b`).
- Strip a trailing separator except for the root itself (`/a/b/` -> `/a/b`, `/` stays `/`).
- Preserve the leading `/` on absolute paths.
- Never touch the filesystem and never consult the current directory.

`is_within` MUST compare whole components, not string prefixes: `is_within("/mnt/bigdata", "/mnt/bigdata2/x")` is `false`. `is_within(root, root)` is `true`.

Append `pub mod normalize;` and `pub use normalize::{component_count, is_within, lexically_normalize, paths_equivalent};` to `paths/mod.rs`.

All tests are inline `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` with `// Arrange` / `// Act` / `// Assert` comments and a message string on every assert.

Implement `CanonicalPath`, the server-side identity of a media file. It is a validated, normalised, absolute UTF-8 path. Every file row, job spec, mount prefix and library root uses this type, so construction must be the only way to get one and must reject anything ambiguous.

Create `crates/transcodarr-core/src/paths/canonical.rs`:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash, serde::Serialize)]
#[serde(transparent)]
pub struct CanonicalPath(camino::Utf8PathBuf);

impl CanonicalPath {
    /// Validate and normalise. Errors on empty, relative, or root-escaping input.
    pub fn new(path: impl AsRef<camino::Utf8Path>) -> Result<Self, PathError>;
    pub fn as_utf8_path(&self) -> &camino::Utf8Path;
    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str;
    pub fn parent(&self) -> Option<CanonicalPath>;
    pub fn file_name(&self) -> Option<&str>;
    /// Join a relative segment, re-validating the result.
    pub fn join(&self, rel: impl AsRef<camino::Utf8Path>) -> Result<Self, PathError>;
    /// Component-wise containment, delegating to `normalize::is_within`.
    pub fn starts_with(&self, prefix: &CanonicalPath) -> bool;
    /// Components of `self` below `prefix`, or None when not contained.
    pub fn strip_prefix(&self, prefix: &CanonicalPath) -> Option<camino::Utf8PathBuf>;
}
```

Validation in `new`: empty input -> `PathError::Empty`; not starting with `/` -> `PathError::NotAbsolute`; a `..` that would rise above the root -> `PathError::TraversalAboveRoot` (note: this differs from `lexically_normalize`, which clamps — `CanonicalPath` rejects instead, so a bad path fails loudly at the boundary). Otherwise store `lexically_normalize(input)`.

Implement `std::fmt::Display` (writes `as_str()`), `AsRef<Utf8Path>`, and a hand-written `Deserialize` that routes through `new` so a malformed string in the DB or on the wire is a deserialisation error rather than a silently-bad path. `TryFrom<&str>` and `TryFrom<String>` with `Error = PathError`.

Append `pub mod canonical;` and `pub use canonical::CanonicalPath;` to `paths/mod.rs`.

Move the two filename-shaping helpers out of the legacy binary into `transcodarr-core`, converting them from `std::path` to `camino::Utf8Path`. Behaviour must be preserved exactly — `strict_stem` splits on the LAST `.` specifically so dotted episode titles such as `Show - 1.11 - Title.mkv` keep their full stem, and there is an existing regression test depending on that.

Source of truth is `/home/jdfalk/repos/github/jdfalk/transcodarr/src/main.rs` lines 185-218 (`suffixed_output`, `strict_stem`). Port them verbatim in behaviour.

Create `crates/transcodarr-core/src/paths/naming.rs`:

```rust
/// Filename stem using the LAST '.' before the extension, so names that
/// legitimately contain dots are not truncated. Dotfiles and extension-less
/// names return the whole file name.
pub fn strict_stem(path: &camino::Utf8Path) -> &str;

/// `<stem>_transcoded.<out_ext>` beside `input_path`.
pub fn suffixed_output(input_path: &camino::Utf8Path, out_ext: &str) -> camino::Utf8PathBuf;
```

`strict_stem` returns a borrowed `&str` tied to the input (the original returned `String`; borrowing is cheaper and the fallback `"output"` is a `'static` literal, so the lifetime works). Preserve the exact fallback chain: file_name + non-empty extension with `rfind(".{ext}")` at position > 0 -> the prefix; else the whole file name; else `"output"`.

`suffixed_output` uses `parent()` falling back to `.` and joins `format!("{stem}_transcoded.{out_ext}")`.

Append `pub mod naming;` and `pub use naming::{strict_stem, suffixed_output};` to `paths/mod.rs`. No filesystem access.

Port the output-path planners into core, removing the `std::env::current_dir()` dependency (src/main.rs:162) that makes the current code unusable on a worker whose cwd differs from the submitter's. The base directory becomes an explicit parameter, and all equivalence checks become lexical (no `canonicalize`).

Create `crates/transcodarr-core/src/paths/output.rs`:

```rust
/// Resolve a safe output path. `base_dir` replaces `env::current_dir()`:
/// a relative `output_opt` is joined onto it. When the resolved output is
/// lexically equivalent to the input, fall back to `suffixed_output` so an
/// in-place overwrite is impossible. `default_ext` defaults to "mkv".
pub fn resolve_output_path(
    input: &camino::Utf8Path,
    output_opt: Option<&camino::Utf8Path>,
    default_ext: Option<&str>,
    base_dir: &camino::Utf8Path,
) -> Result<camino::Utf8PathBuf, PathError>;

/// Mirror `input`'s position under `in_root` into `out_root`, replacing the
/// extension with `ext`. When the two roots are lexically equivalent, returns
/// `suffixed_output(input, ext)` instead so a same-directory batch never
/// overwrites its sources. Extracted from `batch_transcode`.
pub fn plan_output_path(
    input: &camino::Utf8Path,
    in_root: &camino::Utf8Path,
    out_root: &camino::Utf8Path,
    ext: &str,
) -> Result<camino::Utf8PathBuf, PathError>;
```

Rules:
- `resolve_output_path`: a relative `input` is also joined onto `base_dir`. Everything is lexically normalised before comparison. `base_dir` must be absolute -> otherwise `PathError::NotAbsolute`.
- `plan_output_path`: build the output file name as `format!("{}.{}", strict_stem(input), ext)` — NOT `set_extension`, so dotted titles behave identically to `suffixed_output`. If `input` is not under `in_root` (per `normalize::is_within`), return `PathError::OutsideRoot { path, root }`.
- Neither function creates directories, stats anything, or touches the filesystem. Directory creation stays a caller concern — this is what fixes the dry-run-creates-directories bug.

Append `pub mod output;` and `pub use output::{plan_output_path, resolve_output_path};` to `paths/mod.rs`.

Define the agent-side half of the path model: the value types only, no translation logic (that is S2-007).

Create `crates/transcodarr-core/src/paths/mount.rs`:

```rust
/// Path syntax of an agent's operating system.
#[non_exhaustive]
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize)]
#[serde(rename_all = "snake_case")]
pub enum Platform { Linux, Windows }

impl Platform {
    pub const fn separator(self) -> char;   // Linux '/', Windows '\\'
    pub const fn as_str(self) -> &'static str; // "linux" | "windows"
}

/// A path as it exists on one specific agent. Deliberately a plain string:
/// it may be a Windows or UNC path the server cannot parse as a Utf8Path.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize)]
pub struct AgentPath { path: String, platform: Platform }

impl AgentPath {
    pub fn new(path: impl Into<String>, platform: Platform) -> Self;
    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str;
    pub const fn platform(&self) -> Platform;
}

/// One entry of an agent's mount table: where a canonical prefix appears locally.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize)]
pub struct Mount {
    pub canonical_prefix: CanonicalPath,
    pub local_path: String,
    pub platform: Platform,
    pub pool_id: Option<String>,
    pub writable: bool,
}

impl Mount {
    /// `local_path` has any trailing separator stripped. Empty `local_path`
    /// is `PathError::InvalidMount`.
    pub fn new(
        canonical_prefix: CanonicalPath,
        local_path: impl Into<String>,
        platform: Platform,
    ) -> Result<Self, PathError>;
}
```

`Mount::new` defaults `pool_id: None` and `writable: true`. `AgentPath` implements `Display` writing `as_str()`. `Platform` derives `Copy`; `Mount` does not.

Append `pub mod mount;` and `pub use mount::{AgentPath, Mount, Platform};` to `paths/mod.rs`.

Implement the translation itself: canonical <-> agent-local paths, driven by longest-prefix match over an agent's mount table. This is what makes an untranslatable path a dispatch-time ineligibility rather than a runtime ffmpeg failure.

Create `crates/transcodarr-core/src/paths/translate.rs`:

```rust
/// An agent's mount entries, kept ordered by descending prefix specificity.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]
pub struct MountTable { /* private Vec<Mount> */ }

impl MountTable {
    /// Sorts by descending `component_count(canonical_prefix)` so the most
    /// specific mount always wins.
    pub fn from_mounts(mounts: impl IntoIterator<Item = Mount>) -> Self;
    pub fn best_match(&self, path: &CanonicalPath) -> Option<&Mount>;
    pub fn covers(&self, path: &CanonicalPath) -> bool;
    pub fn mounts(&self) -> &[Mount];
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool;
}

/// Translates canonical server paths to agent-local paths and back.
#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default)]
pub struct PathTranslator { /* private MountTable */ }

impl PathTranslator {
    pub fn from_mounts(mounts: &[Mount]) -> Self;
    pub fn to_agent(&self, p: &CanonicalPath) -> Result<AgentPath, PathError>;
    pub fn to_canonical(&self, p: &AgentPath) -> Result<CanonicalPath, PathError>;
    pub fn covers(&self, p: &CanonicalPath) -> bool;
    pub fn mount_table(&self) -> &MountTable;
}
```

Matching rules:
- Containment is component-wise via `CanonicalPath::starts_with`, never a raw string prefix, so `/mnt/bigdata` must NOT match `/mnt/bigdata2/x`.
- When several mounts contain the path, the one with the most prefix components wins. Ties (impossible for distinct valid prefixes) resolve to the first after the stable sort.
- No match -> `PathError::NoMountCovers { path }`.

`to_agent`: take the remainder from `CanonicalPath::strip_prefix(mount.canonical_prefix)`, split it on `/`, and join onto `mount.local_path` using `mount.platform.separator()`. A path exactly equal to the prefix translates to `mount.local_path` alone. Produces an `AgentPath` tagged with the mount's platform.

`to_canonical`: reverse operation. Find the mount whose `local_path` is a component-wise prefix of `p.as_str()` under `p.platform()`'s separator, take the remainder, convert separators to `/`, and join onto `canonical_prefix`. No match -> `PathError::UnknownAgentPath { path }`. A platform mismatch between `p.platform()` and the mount's platform is also `UnknownAgentPath`.

Append `pub mod translate;` and `pub use translate::{MountTable, PathTranslator};` to `paths/mod.rs`.

